<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colonna family</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Colonna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Colonna family is a Roman noble family prominent in the politics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> from the twelfth century. Long rivals of the Orsini, they produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a>, elected in 1417 to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Great Western Schism</a>, as well as many cardinals and commanders, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcantonio-colonna">Marcantonio Colonna</a>, who led the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> at Lepanto in 1571.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colonna-family.jpg" alt="Colonna family" width="320" height="374" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ZuppaDiCarlo: blazon editing, Heralder: subjects · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stemma_della_famiglia_Colonna.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>According to tradition, the Colonna are a branch of the Counts of Tusculum, taking their name from the Columna Castle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alban-hills">Alban Hills</a>, held by Peter de Columna, lord of Colonna, Monteporzio, Zagarolo and Gallicano. From the 14th century the family claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julio-claudian-dynasty">Julio-Claudian dynasty</a>. The first cardinal certainly of the family was Giovanni Colonna di Carbognano, appointed in 1206; an earlier cardinal long counted as a Colonna was shown by modern scholars to have been included on false information from the early 16th century. A rivalry with the pro-papal Orsini, leaders of the Guelph faction, began in this period and reinforced the Colonna's Ghibelline, pro-imperial course.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In 1297 Cardinal Jacopo Colonna disinherited his brothers, who appealed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-boniface-viii">Pope Boniface VIII</a>. When Jacopo refused to return the land and surrender the family strongholds, Boniface removed him from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals">College of Cardinals</a> and excommunicated him and his followers. The Colonna declared that Boniface had been elected illegally after the abdication of Celestine V. By the end of 1298 Landolfo Colonna, commanding the papal army, had razed Colonna, Palestrina and other towns, and the rest of the family fled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><p>The exiles allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-iv-of-france">Philip IV of France</a>. In September 1303 Sciarra Colonna and Guillaume de Nogaret led a small force into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anagni">Anagni</a> to arrest Boniface; Sciarra reportedly slapped the pope, an event called the Outrage of Anagni. Locals freed Boniface, who died on 11 October. In 1328 Sciarra Colonna crowned Louis IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> when John XXII refused, and the family received the right to place the imperial pointed crown above its arms. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> was a friend of the family, especially of Giovanni Colonna, and wrote sonnets for it.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Oddone Colonna</a> was elected on 14 November 1417 as Martin V and reigned until 20 February 1431. The feud with the Orsini was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-julius-ii">Pope Julius II</a>'s bull <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pax-romana">Pax Romana</a> in 1511, and in 1571 the heads of both houses married nieces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-v">Pope Sixtus V</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The family supplied one pope and 23 cardinals. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcantonio-colonna">Marcantonio II Colonna</a> led the papal fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lepanto">Battle of Lepanto</a> on 7 October 1571 and was Viceroy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> from 1577 to 1584. Vittoria Colonna, a sixteenth-century poet, was a friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, and Costanza Colonna was patron and protector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravaggio">Caravaggio</a>. The Colonna were Prince Assistants to the Papal Throne, and the Palazzo Colonna in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> is open to the public.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colonna family” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Henry died in 1216 came Peter of Courtenay, his widow Yolanda of Flanders, the regents Conon de Béthune and Cardinal Giovanni <b>Colonna</b>, Robert of Courtenay, the regent John of Brienne and Baldwin II.</blockquote><a href="/empire/latin-empire">Latin Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Society was dominated by baronial families such as the <b>Colonna</b> and Orsini and by the relatives of successive popes, who acquired land and titles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its exact location was only confirmed in the 19th century; the village of <b>Colonna</b> di Buriano took the ancient name in 1887.</blockquote><a href="/state/vetulonia">Vetulonia</a></li></ul></section>
